  author: "Jackie Bruce",
  faqs: [
    {
      q: "What is Greenwich Mean Time (GMT)?",
      a: "Greenwich Mean Time (GMT) is the time standard centered on the Prime Meridian passing through the Royal Observatory in Greenwich, London, United Kingdom. It represents a zero time offset (UTC+0). Historically, GMT served as the international civil time standard based on Earth's solar rotation before being superseded by Coordinated Universal Time (UTC) for scientific timekeeping."
    },
    {
      q: "Is GMT exact same as UTC?",
      a: "While GMT and UTC share the exact same temporal offset (UTC+0) for civil and everyday scheduling, they are distinct standards. GMT is a physical, astronomical time zone based on solar observations at the Greenwich Meridian. UTC (Coordinated Universal Time) is a high-precision atomic time standard regulated by International Atomic Time (TAI) and adjusted with leap seconds to remain synchronized with Earth's rotation."
    },
    {
      q: "Does Greenwich Mean Time (GMT) observe Daylight Saving Time?",
      a: "GMT itself is a standard time offset (UTC+0) and never changes or observes Daylight Saving Time. However, countries that use GMT during the winter months—such as the United Kingdom and Ireland—shift to daylight saving time in the summer (British Summer Time, UTC+1, or Irish Standard Time, UTC+1). Other regions, such as Iceland and parts of West Africa, observe GMT (UTC+0) year-round without clock shifts."
    },
    {
      q: "Which countries observe GMT year-round?",
