    },
    {
      q: "What is the difference between GMT and EST?",
      a: "Greenwich Mean Time (GMT) is UTC+0, while Eastern Standard Time (EST) is UTC-5. This creates a 5-hour time difference: GMT is 5 hours ahead of EST. When it is 12:00 PM (noon) GMT, it is 7:00 AM EST in New York or Toronto. During North American daylight saving time (EDT, UTC-4), the gap narrows to 4 hours."
    },
    {
      q: "Why is GMT called 'Mean' time?",
      a: "The word 'mean' in Greenwich Mean Time refers to the mathematical average. Because Earth's orbit around the Sun is elliptical and its axis is tilted, actual solar noon fluctuates slightly throughout the year (the Equation of Time). GMT averages these annual solar variations to create a uniform, 24-hour day based on a fictitious 'mean Sun'."
    },
    {
      q: "What is Zulu Time and how does it relate to GMT?",
      a: "Zulu time (Z) is the military and aviation designation for UTC+0 / GMT. The letter 'Z' corresponds to the zero-meridian time zone in the NATO phonetic alphabet ('Zulu'). Pilots, meteorologists, and global military operations use Zulu time to prevent confusion across international airspace."
    },
    {
      q: "When did GMT become the global time standard?",
      a: "GMT was adopted as the national standard time for Great Britain by railway companies in 1847 and established by UK law in 1880. Internationally, the 1884 International Meridian Conference in Washington, D.C. selected the Greenwich Observatory as the official Prime Meridian (0° longitude) for global navigational and timezone referencing."
    },
    {
      q: "What is the time difference between GMT and PST?",
      a: "GMT (UTC+0) is 8 hours ahead of Pacific Standard Time (PST, UTC-8). When it is 4:00 PM GMT, it is 8:00 AM PST in Los Angeles, Seattle, or Vancouver. During Pacific Daylight Time (PDT, UTC-7), GMT is 7 hours ahead."
    },
    {
      q: "How do I convert GMT to Eastern Standard Time (EST)?",
      a: "To convert GMT to EST, subtract 5 hours from the GMT time. For example, 15:00 GMT minus 5 hours equals 10:00 EST. If calculating during US summer (EDT), subtract 4 hours instead."
    },
    {
      q: "How does GMT affect global financial markets?",
      a: "GMT serves as a key reference for European trading hours. The London Stock Exchange (LSE) opens at 08:00 GMT (08:00 BST in summer) and closes at 16:30 GMT. Global forex markets track trading desk handovers from Tokyo to London (GMT) and New York."
    },
    {
      q: "What is the difference between GMT and BST?",
      a: "GMT is Greenwich Mean Time (UTC+0), used during the UK winter months (late October to late March). BST is British Summer Time (UTC+1), used during the summer months when clocks spring forward by 1 hour."
    },
    {
      q: "Does London always run on GMT?",
      a: "No. London operates on GMT (UTC+0) only during autumn and winter. On the last Sunday of March, London advances its clocks by one hour to British Summer Time (BST, UTC+1). It returns to GMT on the last Sunday of October."
    },
    {
      q: "How is GMT formatted in computer programming and HTTP headers?",
      a: "In software engineering and web standards (such as RFC 7231 for HTTP headers), GMT is widely used as a textual timestamp representation (e.g., 'Wed, 21 Oct 2026 07:28:00 GMT'). In modern codebases, UTC is preferred for internal timestamps."
    },
    {
      q: "What is the GMT offset for Central European Time (CET)?",
      a: "Central European Time (CET) is UTC+1, which is 1 hour ahead of GMT (UTC+0). When it is 12:00 PM GMT in London (winter), it is 1:00 PM CET in Paris, Berlin, and Rome."
    },
    {
      q: "What is the GMT offset for India Standard Time (IST)?",
      a: "India Standard Time (IST) is UTC+5:30, which is 5 hours and 30 minutes ahead of GMT. When it is 12:00 PM GMT, it is 5:30 PM IST in New Delhi and Mumbai."
    },
    {
      q: "What is the GMT offset for Australian Eastern Standard Time (AEST)?",
      a: "Australian Eastern Standard Time (AEST) is UTC+10, making it 10 hours ahead of GMT. When it is 8:00 AM GMT, it is 6:00 PM AEST in Sydney, Melbourne, and Brisbane."
    },
    {
      q: "How does GPS time relate to GMT?",
      a: "GPS time is an atomic time scale managed by the US Space Force. It was synchronized with UTC/GMT in 1980. However, unlike UTC, GPS time does not incorporate leap seconds, making it currently 18 seconds ahead of UTC/GMT."
    },
    {
      q: "Is Greenwich Mean Time used in maritime navigation?",
      a: "Yes. Marine chronometers set to GMT/UTC have been fundamental to calculating longitude at sea since the 18th century. Navigators compare local solar time with GMT chronometers to pinpoint exact vessel positions."
    },
    {
      q: "Can GMT offset be positive or negative?",
      a: "GMT itself is the zero meridian baseline (UTC+0). Other world time zones are expressed as positive offsets (+1 to +14 hours east of Greenwich) or negative offsets (-1 to -12 hours west of Greenwich)."
    }
  ]
};

