    <section id="what-is-gmt" class="content-section">
      <h2>1. What is Greenwich Mean Time (GMT)?</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Greenwich Mean Time (GMT)</strong> is the clock time baseline calculated at the Royal Observatory in Greenwich, London, located precisely on the Prime Meridian (0° longitude). Established as the foundation of international standard time, GMT has a perpetual offset of <strong>UTC+0</strong>. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Before the invention of electronic telegraphy and widespread railroad networks in the 19th century, every city kept its own local solar time based on when the Sun crossed its local meridian. This led to hundreds of conflicting local clocks across short distances. In 1847, the Railway Clearing House in Great Britain mandated Greenwich Mean Time across all British train tracks to establish a unified rail schedule, giving rise to the term "Railway Time."
      </p>
      <p>
        By 1880, GMT became the official legal time of Great Britain. Four years later, at the 1884 International Meridian Conference in Washington, D.C., 25 nations voted to adopt the Greenwich Meridian as the international <strong>Prime Meridian (0° longitude)</strong>, establishing GMT as the global standard reference point from which all world time zones were calculated.
      </p>

      <div class="callout callout-tip">
        <h3>Key Takeaway: Baseline Meridian</h3>
        <p>
          Because GMT is located at 0° longitude, all global time zones are measured by how many hours they are ahead of GMT (e.g., <a href="/cet/">CET UTC+1</a>, <a href="/ist/">IST UTC+5:30</a>, <a href="/aest/">AEST UTC+10</a>) or behind GMT (e.g., <a href="/est/">EST UTC-5</a>, <a href="/cst/">CST UTC-6</a>, <a href="/pst/">PST UTC-8</a>).
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

