    <section id="standards-and-systems" class="content-section">
      <h2>6. GMT in Aviation, Maritime, and Software Architecture</h2>
      <p>
        Because of its legacy as the prime zero baseline, GMT (and its atomic equivalent UTC) plays an essential role across global industries:
      </p>
      <div class="grid-two-col">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Aviation & Military: Zulu Time</h3>
          <p>
            In international aviation, flight plans, air traffic control coordinates, and pilot logs are stamped in <strong>Zulu Time (Z)</strong>. Zulu is the phonetic alphabet term for zero, referencing 0° longitude GMT. Using a single zero-offset standard prevents catastrophic timing misunderstandings when aircraft cross multiple international boundaries.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>HTTP Headers & Web Specifications</h3>
          <p>
            The official HTTP protocol standard (<a href="/iso-8601-date-time-format/">RFC 7231</a>) mandates that web servers transmit date headers using GMT strings (e.g., <code>Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 07:28:00 GMT</code>). While database backends store epoch numbers via <a href="/unix-time-explained/">Unix timestamps</a>, HTTP response payloads maintain GMT strings for backward compatibility.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="business-and-finance" class="content-section">
      <h2>7. Financial Operations & Cross-Border Scheduling</h2>
      <p>
        London is one of the world's primary financial centers. The <strong>London Stock Exchange (LSE)</strong> opens its trading session at 08:00 GMT (08:00 BST in summer) and closes at 16:30 GMT. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Because London's business day overlaps with both Asian trading closing hours (Tokyo/Hong Kong) and North American opening hours (<a href="/new-york-time/">New York Stock Exchange</a>), GMT acts as the bridge for global currency (Forex) liquidity handovers. Distributed international teams regularly schedule cross-continental syncs around the 14:00–16:00 GMT window to accommodate participants from London, New York, and San Francisco simultaneously. For more scheduling strategies, view our guide on <a href="/remote-team-scheduling/">remote team scheduling</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

